<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Nasi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as João Miques</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Nasi was a Portuguese-born Jewish financier and diplomat who rose to great influence at the Ottoman court under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a>. In 1566 the sultan made him duke of Naxos and the Archipelago after deposing its last Crispo duke, and he ruled the islands through a deputy. He also sponsored Jewish resettlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nasi was born in Portugal as a marrano, practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> in secret, the son of Agostinho Micas, a physician and professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. He belonged to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> and was a nephew of Doña Gracia Mendes Nasi. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> began acting against Portuguese marranos in 1546 he moved with his aunt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in the Habsburg Netherlands, studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a> and fled the Inquisition in 1547. By way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> he reached the Ottoman realm in 1554, where he married his cousin, a daughter of Gracia Mendes Nasi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he backed the future sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> against his rival Bayezid and so won favour at the Seraglio, becoming a high-ranking diplomat. His trading links in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> gave him great influence on Ottoman foreign policy: he negotiated peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, held monopolies of the beeswax trade with Poland and the wine trade with Moldavia, and worked to keep princes favourable to his policies in power in Moldavia, among them Ioan Iacob Heraclid in 1561. In 1571 he was himself proposed as hospodar of Moldavia or Wallachia, but Selim II refused.</p><p>When his dealings with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Venetian Cyprus were uncovered, most of the Jews of Famagusta were expelled in June 1568. He is believed to have urged the Ottoman conquest of the island in the hope of a Jewish colony there, and he kept contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, encouraging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1561, with his aunt, he undertook to resettle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a>, the first attempt in modern times to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in what was then Ottoman Syria by practical means rather than awaiting the Messiah. With Joseph ben Adruth he rebuilt Tiberias's walls and planted mulberry trees for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, but the plan was abandoned when war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> began. He ruled Naxos mainly from his palace of Belvedere near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he kept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> press. After Selim's death he lost influence but kept his titles and pension until he died in 1579.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Nasi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jews, Greeks and Armenians were prominent in trade and finance; the Jewish financier <b>Joseph Nasi</b> rose so high at court that Selim II made him duke of Naxos in 1566.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1566 the Ottomans deposed Giacomo IV Crispo and gave the duchy to the Jewish financier <b>Joseph Nasi</b>, who ruled through a deputy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-archipelago">Duchy of the Archipelago</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1559" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Nasi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1559">What was here in 1559? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
